M2 notes are proposed designs, with shared Spark host resources and independent lab histories. Earlier M1 carrier-pilot and generic-host ideas remain history; they do not redefine this milestone or establish runtime evidence.

## Durable project labs

[[Designs/Durable Project Labs]] owns long-lived lab identity, model changes, persisted knowledge/data, recovery, bounded project-level improvement and presentation checkpoints. Read it before VM lifecycle and storage implementation. Existing M2 topology and host resource policies remain linked owners.

## Nuwa briefing

[[Designs/Nuwa Briefing/presentation/README|Nuwa rev3]] uses an orange person-to-skill narrative to assess value to AI4R: what to imitate in evidence-based procedure extraction, what pitfalls to avoid, a proposed M2 CC-factory branch, and a generic-checklist comparison. Nine main slides and two hidden backups, planned 7:20. The latest version adds method/criteria tables and three CC-factory inputs: runtime gap, paper/repo/data and person-plus-trait. Luna adversarially reviewed and spot-checked the revisions. No integration or task gain is claimed.


## M2 lab capsules and factory proposal

[[Designs/M2 Lab Capsules and Factory Proposal]] records October 26 ownership and local-adoption decisions, multi-operation packages, simulator service/data placement, factory profiles and a proposed adaptive-planning interface. Whole-system RSI remains with its owner. It is a discussion proposal beneath the high-level lab purpose, not a runtime result or an amendment to M1.
